//!
//! Three metrics quantify detection significance. The **detection SNR** measures excess power at the predicted wavenumber relative to the spectral noise floor. A detection requires SNR > 2, corresponding to a one-sided false-positive rate of approximately 2.3% under Gaussian noise assumptions; this threshold is standard in spectral searches where the wavenumber is predicted a priori. The **ZD amplitude** $\alpha_{\text{zd}} = \text{SNR} / (0.5\,e)$ normalizes detection SNR to a dimensionless measure of halo perturbation strength; because this is a linear transformation of detection SNR, both metrics carry identical statistical information. The **success rate** tracks the fraction of analyses completing without numerical failure.
//!
//! All experiments were executed on a single CPU core (AMD Ryzen 9 7950X, 5.7 GHz boost clock) with 64 GB DDR5 RAM. The complete experimental matrix of 660 analyses used deterministic initialization via controlled random seeds, ensuring exact bitwise reproducibility across runs. The pipeline achieved 100% completion with zero numerical failures across all configurations.
//!
//! # Results
//!
//! Table 3 presents the detection SNR for all eleven experimental conditions, computed as grand means across three subsamples and twenty seeds per condition (660 total cells, 100% success rate). While eleven conditions are enumerated, the effective number of independent measurements is six to eight: the four algebraic framework conditions are quasi-degenerate at MaNGA radii ($\rho > 0.97$ for all six pairwise correlations), contributing approximately one independent constraint rather than four. The ablation, control, and injection conditions provide genuinely independent tests.
//!
