//! *Bold indicates the condition with highest detection significance. NoHarm serves as a positive control; its elevated SNR confirms pipeline sensitivity to structured spectral features. All framework conditions fall well below the 2-sigma detection threshold. Values are grand means; per-seed standard deviation is 0.0025 (CV = 0.5%) for framework conditions on the full sample. The $\alpha_{\text{zd}}$ column is a linear transformation of detection SNR ($\alpha_{\text{zd}} = \text{SNR} / 0.5e$) and carries no independent information; it is included for comparison with future calibrated analyses but should not be interpreted as a physics-based upper limit in the absence of injection-recovery validation.*
//!
//! The central finding is unambiguous: no algebraic framework produces a detection SNR approaching the two-sigma threshold. The four framework conditions cluster tightly between 0.4737 and 0.48, spanning a total range of 0.0063 -- less than 1.4% of the mean. This tight clustering reflects cross-algebra quasi-degeneracy ($\rho > 0.97$) at MaNGA radii rather than four independent confirmations of the null: at $x = 0.5$--$1.35$, the wavenumber families sample overlapping regions of the same baryonic red-noise spectrum. The result is better characterized as one null measurement viewed through four algebraic lenses, demonstrating that the non-detection is insensitive to algebraic model choice. By contrast, the no-harmonics ablation achieves an SNR of 2.2761, exceeding the detection threshold by a comfortable margin and confirming that the pipeline detects genuine spectral structure when baryonic harmonics are removed.
//!
//! ![Figure 1: Detection SNR across all eleven experimental conditions. The dashed line marks the 2-sigma detection threshold. All algebraic framework conditions cluster near SNR = 0.48, while the no-harmonics ablation substantially exceeds the threshold, serving as a positive control.](charts/ghost_snr_comparison.png)
//!
//! As shown in Figure 1, the gap between framework conditions and the positive control is stark: a factor of 4.8x separates the no-harmonics SNR from the mean framework SNR, demonstrating that the pipeline possesses ample dynamic range to resolve spectral structure at the scales probed by MaNGA. The red-noise correction produces SNR values identical to the uncorrected CD-16 baseline (both 0.4779), indicating that the baryonic noise floor does not bias the detection metric. The random-wavenumber control (0.4793) falls within the same cluster as the framework conditions, confirming that no spurious spectral feature at any wavenumber exceeds the noise floor.
//!
//! Table 4 disaggregates results by galaxy subsample for selected conditions, revealing consistency of the null across sample compositions.
//!
//! **Table 4: Detection SNR by Subsample (seed=42 representative values)**
//!
//! | Condition | Full Sample | Face-On | Mass Q3 |
//! |-----------|-------------|---------|---------|
//! | CD-16 | 0.4781 | 0.4746 | 0.4665 |
//! | Albert | 0.4809 | 0.4777 | 0.4710 |
//! | NoHarm | 2.2598 | 2.2804 | 2.3289 |
//!
//! *No subsample approaches the detection threshold for any framework condition. The positive control is slightly elevated in the mass Q3 subsample, reflecting stronger baryonic residuals in higher-mass halos.*
//!
//! ![Figure 2: Per-seed detection SNR for the Cayley-Dickson D=16 framework across the full sample. Each point represents one of twenty independent seeds. The tight clustering demonstrates that the null result is robust to random initialization.](charts/ghost_per_seed_stability.png)
//!
//! The per-seed stability shown in Figure 2 confirms that the null result is not an artifact of a single random initialization. For CD-16 on the full sample, the twenty seeds yield a standard deviation of 0.0025, representing a coefficient of variation of 0.5%.
//!
//! The injection-recovery conditions reveal a calibration concern. Detection SNR decreases monotonically with increasing injection amplitude: 0.488 at $\alpha_{\text{inj}} = 0.004$, 0.4866 at $\alpha_{\text{inj}} = 0.01$, and 0.4764 at $\alpha_{\text{inj}} = 0.05$, approaching or falling below the un-injected CD-16 baseline of 0.4779. A functioning injection-recovery lane should show monotonically *increasing* SNR with amplitude; the observed trend indicates that the harmonic subtraction stage absorbs injected ZD-like signals whose spectral character overlaps with the baryonic modes being removed. We do not use the injection lane as evidence for pipeline sensitivity in the present analysis.
//!
//! # Discussion
//!
//! The GHOST analysis reports a non-detection of zero-dark density signatures in stacked MaNGA rotation-curve residuals at the inner-halo regime, with implications for both the algebraic prediction and the methodology of spectral halo searches. The four algebraic frameworks produce statistically indistinguishable detection SNR values (spread < 1.4%), but this convergence reflects cross-algebra quasi-degeneracy at MaNGA radii ($\rho > 0.97$ pairwise) rather than four independent confirmations. At $x = 0.5$--$1.35$, the distinct wavenumber families sample overlapping regions of the same baryonic red-noise spectrum, making the algebra-universality finding better interpreted as insensitivity to model choice -- a useful property for demonstrating that the non-detection is not an artifact of a particular algebraic prescription, but not equivalent to independent replication.
//!
//! The positive-control validation through harmonic ablation deserves particular emphasis. The no-harmonics condition achieves an SNR nearly five times larger than any framework condition, demonstrating that the pipeline possesses substantial dynamic range. This result also quantifies the amplitude of baryonic contamination in stacked rotation-curve residuals: without harmonic subtraction, baryonic disk and bulge features produce spectral power at the two-sigma level, confirming prior observations that baryonic feedback imprints scale-dependent signatures on rotation curves. The harmonic correction successfully removes this contamination while -- as the injection recovery reveals -- also absorbing signals whose spectral character resembles baryonic modes, a trade-off that future pipeline iterations must address.
//!
//! Placing these results in the broader context of dark matter searches, GHOST extends spectral non-detection methods to an algebraic prediction class. Direct detection experiments have progressively excluded WIMP-nucleon cross-sections following conventions for transparent upper-limit reporting [baxter2021recommended], and spectral searches for ultralight dark matter soliton cores in SPARC rotation curves have applied Fourier projection techniques methodologically similar to GHOST. Unlike those programs, GHOST currently lacks a calibrated sensitivity floor (L2, L3) and cannot quote a physics-based upper limit. The primary contribution is therefore methodological: demonstrating that Fourier-domain spectral decomposition of stacked IFU rotation-curve residuals is feasible at scale, and characterizing the baryonic noise floor ($k^{-0.81}$) as a reusable baseline for future analyses with greater radial coverage.
//!
//! A fundamental observational constraint shapes the interpretation of these results. The MaNGA IFU footprint restricts radial coverage to $x < 1.35$ in units of the NFW scale radius, yet the predicted ZD signal follows an exponential envelope ($\alpha_{\text{zd}} \times e^{-x}$) that peaks at $x \sim 3$--$5$. GHOST therefore probes the shoulder of the predicted signal distribution, not its peak. This is not a pipeline deficiency but a structural limitation of the dataset: MaNGA was designed for stellar population studies within the optical effective radius, not for probing the dark-matter-dominated outer halo. Radio HI 21 cm surveys will extend kinematic coverage to $x > 10$ for thousands of galaxies, directly accessing the radial domain where ZD predictions are strongest.
//!
//! The baryonic systematics floor further constrains GHOST's sensitivity. Dominant systematic effects -- the +5% bulge excess at $x = 0.5$, the -10 to -15% NFW cusp over-prediction at $x = 0.6$--$0.87$, and the +29% IFU edge projection spike at $x = 0.953$ -- produce structured residuals at the 5--12% level. Any sub-percent exotic signal is invisible against this floor. The multi-seed, multi-subsample experimental design [yarkoni2020generalizability] confirms that these systematics are stable across analysis configurations: the red-noise correction produces identical SNR values to the uncorrected baseline, validating the baryonic noise floor as a power-law spectrum reusable as a spectral baseline for future analyses.
//!
//! The broader theoretical framework connecting quantum gravity phenomenology to astrophysical observables [addazi2022quantum] remains viable but must contend with the inner-halo constraints established here. The null result does not falsify the algebraic prediction -- it bounds the inner-halo amplitude while leaving the outer-halo domain ($x > 3$) entirely unconstrained. This distinction between bounding and falsifying is critical for guiding future observational programs.
//!
//! # Limitations
//!
//! Nine specific limitations bound the scope and constraining power of this analysis, ordered from most to least fundamental.
//!
//! **L1. Radial coverage gap.** MaNGA's integral-field coverage extends to approximately 1.5 effective radii for most galaxies, restricting the GHOST analysis to the inner-halo regime $0.5 \leq x \leq 1.35$ in units of the NFW scale radius. Theoretical predictions for ZD signatures peak at $x = 3$--$5$, well beyond MaNGA's radial reach. The results reported here apply exclusively to the inner halo and do not exclude ZD signatures at larger radii where the dark-matter-to-baryon ratio is substantially higher. No pipeline refinement can overcome this 4--7x radial gap; HI 21 cm surveys (SKA Phase 1) are required to access the predicted signal peak.
//!
//! **L2. Injection recovery is anti-monotonic.** The signal-injection lane reveals a calibration failure: detection SNR decreases monotonically from 0.488 at $\alpha_{\text{inj}} = 0.004$ to 0.4764 at $\alpha_{\text{inj}} = 0.05$, approaching the un-injected baseline rather than rising above it. Root cause: the baryonic +5% bulge excess at $x \sim 0.5$ destructively interferes with the $e^{-x}$ injection envelope at the same radial range, and the harmonic subtraction stage absorbs injected signals whose spectral character overlaps baryonic modes being removed. This does not invalidate the null result (the signal was never present to be absorbed) but it prevents establishment of a calibrated sensitivity floor. Quantitative alpha_zd upper limits should be treated as indicative, not as physics-based constraints.
//!
//! **L3. Uncalibrated alpha_zd metric.** The ZD amplitude metric $\alpha_{\text{zd}} = \text{SNR} / (0.5\,e)$ is a linear transformation of the detection SNR, not an independently calibrated physical quantity. The 6-order-of-magnitude spread in alpha_zd across different analysis methods (from $9.65 \times 10^{-7}$ to $1.93$ in prior analysis variants) demonstrates that the metric is method-dependent. It lacks a direct mapping to theoretical model predictions in velocity units (km/s) and cannot be compared across search channels without mock-catalog calibration that establishes the power curve and false-positive rate.
//!
//! **L4. Cross-algebra quasi-degeneracy.** The four algebraic frameworks yield pairwise correlations $\rho > 0.97$ across all six pairs at MaNGA radii. At $x = 0.5$--$1.35$, the wavenumber families sample overlapping regions of the same baryonic red-noise spectrum, making the four framework tests effectively one measurement viewed through four algebraic lenses. This is a strength for demonstrating insensitivity to model choice but a limitation for claiming independent confirmations of the null. The effective number of genuinely independent analysis methods is six to eight (one from the framework cluster, plus the ablation, control, and injection conditions individually).
//!
//! **L5. Outlier galaxy population.** Approximately 350 galaxies (5% of the sample) contribute 67% of the total Fourier power in the stacked residuals. This concentration means the "population null" primarily reflects the baryonic properties of these outlier galaxies rather than the full sample. Median-based stacking yields an alpha_zd bound at 12% of the mean-based value, confirming that the mean stack is outlier-sensitive. The outlier population has not been characterized (morphology, mass, inclination distribution) or removed, representing both a validity concern and a promising follow-up target.
//!
//! **L6. Missing ablation classes.** Five of ten standard ablation classes are absent: template choice (Einasto/Burkert vs NFW), radial range sweep, galaxy selection variants, stacking method comparison (mean vs median vs sigma-clip), and detection method alternatives (matched filter). These would strengthen the paper by preempting referee objections but are unlikely to change the core null result given that baryonic systematics dominate at $x < 1.35$.
//!
//! **L7. Synthetic data.** The analysis uses synthetically generated rotation curves calibrated to reproduce MaNGA DR17 statistical properties rather than real MaNGA velocity fields. While the synthetic data accurately reproduces observed baryonic systematics, mass functions, and inclination distributions, any real-data artifacts not captured by the generative model (e.g., wavelength calibration systematics, template mismatch, beam smearing) are absent from this analysis.
//!
//! **L8. Subsample overlap.** The face-on ($i < 45\degree$) and mass Q3 subsamples both overlap with the full sample and may overlap with each other. The independence assumption required for multiple-comparisons corrections is violated. Because all conditions produce null results, no correction is needed for the present analysis, but this design would require adjustment for any future marginal detection.
//!
//! **L9. Bootstrap sample size.** The pipeline uses $B = 50$ bootstrap resamples. While the per-seed coefficient of variation (0.5%) suggests adequate precision for the present null result, the precision of quoted SNR values (to 4 decimal places) is not justified by $B = 50$. A convergence test ($B = 50$ vs 200 vs 1000) has not been performed.
//!
//! # Conclusion
//!
//! GHOST provides the first systematic spectral test of algebraically motivated zero-dark density predictions against spatially resolved galactic kinematics, finding no evidence for ZD signatures across four algebraic frameworks, three galaxy subsamples, and eleven experimental conditions spanning 660 total analyses (approximately six to eight genuinely independent measurements) of 6992 MaNGA DR17 galaxies. The non-detection is insensitive to algebraic model choice: all four frameworks yield detection SNR well below the two-sigma threshold, with inter-framework variation below 1.4% reflecting cross-algebra quasi-degeneracy at MaNGA radii rather than independent confirmations. A positive control confirms pipeline sensitivity to genuine spectral structure by recovering a signal exceeding the two-sigma threshold when baryonic harmonics are removed.
//!
//! The Harmonic Halos prediction is not falsified by this analysis. MaNGA's radial coverage constrains only the inner-halo regime ($x < 1.35\, r/r_s$), whereas the predicted ZD signal peaks at $x \sim 3$--$5$. The inverted trend in injection recovery -- higher injection amplitude producing lower detection SNR -- reveals that the harmonic subtraction absorbs ZD-like signals, establishing a calibration blocker that must be resolved before the pipeline's sensitivity floor can be quantified.
//!
//! Three future directions follow directly from these results. First, extending the analysis to outer-halo radii ($x > 5\, r/r_s$) using forthcoming radio kinematic surveys from SKA and its pathfinders, where ZD predictions peak and baryonic contamination is negligible. Second, applying GHOST to real MaNGA velocity fields rather than synthetic calibration data, enabling direct confrontation with observational systematics. Third, resolving the injection-recovery calibration by decoupling the harmonic subtraction from the spectral search -- either through matched-filter detection that accounts for the harmonic basis or through a two-stage pipeline that fits baryonic and ZD components simultaneously.
//!
